## What the smoke test draws on

The cases run on Bun's test runner, and Bun spawns the executable and `tar`, gzips the stand-in executables, and serves the stand-in registry and release. Node's modules make the folders and files a case needs, copy the executable to upgrade, compute a checksum, and turn paths into the `file:` addresses the language server names documents by. Volar's language-server package carries the client half of the protocol: `createMessageConnection`, the stream reader and writer it speaks through, and the `TextEdit` a format answers with. The language store names the TypeScript service's package and this Loom's version, and the host runtime names the key under which the stand-in finds Effect.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import {
  createMessageConnection,
  StreamMessageReader,
  StreamMessageWriter,
  type TextEdit,
} from '@volar/language-server/node'
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process'
import { createHash } from 'node:crypto'
import {
  chmodSync,
  copyFileSync,
  existsSync,
  mkdirSync,
  mkdtempSync,
  readFileSync,
  realpathSync,
  rmSync,
  writeFileSync,
} from 'node:fs'
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os'
import { basename, dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'
import { servicePackage, serviceVersion } from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LoomStore'
import { runtimeKey } from '@athrio/loom-lang-services/Runtime'

